 to  the star wars movies at the entrance to  his home there's a periscope scanner  presumably a security protocol to alert  him to intruders  fans may recognize the device's design  as an identical periscope is used  repeatedly by r2d2 throughout the star  wars films hopefully obi-wans are simply  a repurposed gadget and not the  appendage of some poor astro megdroid  burrowed in the sun  later in episode 1 obi-wan takes out an  old hologram communicator to receive a  transmission from bale or ghana it's  another device that should be familiar  to those who have seen the movies as  obi-wan is seen using it in the prequel  trilogy perhaps most striking of all  however is the cloud of defeat that  follows obi-wan under his bend persona  the jedi truly looks like he's given up  he's buried his and anakin's lightsaber  in the desert in what is a possible  allusion to the end of the rise of  skywalker he's terrorized by nightmares  and he is hesitant to use the force in  basically any scenario given how calm  and controlled obi-wan is in a new hope  it's interesting to see him in such a  dark place  if you've absorbed much star wars media  in the disney era then you're probably  familiar with the imperial inquisitors  moses ingram's reaver who takes a  villainous lead in the disney plus  series is a new addition but some of the  emperor's agents have appeared in the  past sung kang's fifth brother for  instance has featured in several  previous projects including the darth  vader comics and star wars rebels he  eventually meets his end at the hands of  darth maul a curious twist to fate given  their shared history of hunting for  obi-wan the grand inquisitor another  prominent villain from the star wars  comics and star wars rebels also makes  his presence felt in the obi-wan show  however he's a bit different this time  around in star wars rebels the grand  inquisitor is voiced by jason isaacs and  some fans were upset when he was recast  with rupert friend for the live-action  version after being stabbed by reaver in  a surprising betrayal though the grand  inquisitor's fade seems up in the air  it's possible that he'll survive the  attack but it's also possible that  friend's character is a different  version of the villain and the isaac's  rendition in rebels might be some sort  of clone it is star wars after all  regardless both the fifth brother and  the grand inquisitor wield the same  spinning lightsabers first shown in star  wars rebels and they look a bit more  intimidating in live action  one of the more touching moments in  obi-wan kenobi episode 1 comes during  ben's interaction with tikka the jawa he  spends a good portion of his hard-earned  money to buy a toy for the young luke  skywalker and not just any toy either  specifically obi-wan purchases a model  of a t-16 skyhopper a small airspeeder  first mentioned in the original star  wars luke references the craft during  the briefing for the first death star  attack claiming that but it's not  impossible i used to bullseye womp rats  in my t-16 back home he's also shown  playing with a toy version of the  speeder during an early conversation  with c-3po it seems that obi-wan was the  giver of that gift all along which is a  fun little twist on a tiny piece of  pre-existing lore though luke's uncle  owen insists that obi-wan takes the toy  back it seems that he acquiesces in the  end  a lot of star wars media has dealt with  luke's time on tatooine but far less  time has been spent exploring his sister  leia's upbringing on old iran obi-wan  kenobi addresses that by spending time  on the planet following a ten-year-old  layer through the ins and outs of her  real life young star vivian lyra blair  does an excellent job bringing the young  princess to life complete with leia's  signature snark stubborn nurse and wet  in one scene while climbing a tree with  her little droid friend lola leia starts  naming the ships she sees leaving the  local spaceport  napoleon ranger probably scouting for  murson pirates the mention of aquilian  rangers is a deep cut reference to the  aquilae first mentioned in an infamous  cut line from the original star wars the  sun pirates appear in some early star  wars comics that have since been  relegated to the legends timeline though  they have also been referenced in  canonical media it's also worth noting  that in an attempt to avoid her adoptive  parents impending political reception  leia sends a young decoy to be dressed  and ornamented in her place the exact  same tactic her mother padme used many  times throughout her political career  leia quickly reminds obi-wan of padme  after they meet and it's clear why  soon after arriving on the crime-ridden  world of daiyu obi-wan sees a face he  certainly didn't expect  that of an old clone trooper begging for  money on the side of a dirty street  though the former republic soldier is  technically much younger than obi-wan he  looks notably older as his body has  broken down due to the camino wins  accelerated aging alterations  it's a powerful reminder of what  happened to the clones after the rise of  the empire as shown in star wars the bad  batch most were quickly phased out of  military service it's clearly jarring  for obi-wan to see the old clone which  makes sense giving his complicated  history with them on the one hand he  probably saw his old friends cody and  rex in the beggar's face and he felt a  deep sadness on the other hand the  events of order 66 would surely have  created a traumatic connection to the  clones obi-wan moves on from the  encounter quickly but it surely stays  with him especially since the clone's  blue accented armor is remarkably  similar to rexus  if there's underworld activity happening  in a star wars story there's a good  chance it has something to do with spice  the all-purpose drug signifier taken  straight from june that rule holds true  in obi-wan kenobi as well with the spice  trade rearing its head immediately on  dayu a young dealer tries to sell the  rogue jedi some of the stuff naming a  few different varieties including kessel  a primary space planet most famous for  the kessel run and felucian a reference  to the planet felucia where jedi ailer  sakura meta and fortune attend drawing  order 66  later on obi-wan infiltrates a spice lab  and even uses a vial of the stuff to  fend off a group of would-be captors he  smashes a small glass container on the  ground and locks his attackers in with  it  sending them all on what looks to be a  pretty intense trip  less on easter egg than a fun little  detail this one's still worth mentioning  given the main character in the series  in the original star wars ben kenobi  describes lightsabers in an interesting  way an elegant weapon  for a more  civilized day  this line is referenced again in revenge  of the sith after obi-wan discards her  used blaster and declares it so  uncivilized it's interesting then that  he uses blasters so prevalently in the  first couple of episodes of his own show  as he doesn't want to out himself as a  jedi obi-wan hesitates to use either the  force or his lightsaber he instead  relies on his martial arts prowess and  what weapons he can find to keep the bad  guys at bay more importantly obi-wan  doesn't complain once about the blasters  he's forced to use almost as if he's had  to let go of his old jedi pride it's a  small detail but it helps build the  sense that he's really given up all hope  if obi-wan kenobi can use a blaster  without a snide comment things truly  have taken a dark turn  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  obi-wan kenobi are coming soon subscribe  to our youtube channel and hit the bell  so you don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

